  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,\u27f9\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098447/assets/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)",
